message.content:
Read-only audit in the repo at /Users/arijit/Coding/Python/DataQ (GitHub repo TheurgicDuke771/DataQ; `gh` CLI is authenticated). Do NOT modify anything (no edits, no gh writes, no comments).

Context: the maintainer is about to tear down their OWN paid dev/test cloud estate: AWS (account 251783195294, us-east-2: ECS/RDS/ElastiCache/CloudFront/WAF/Cognito/Secrets Manager/SSM/SES/Bedrock/S3/Athena/Redshift), Azure (resource group dataq-rg: Container Apps, Postgres, Key Vault, ADF `dataq-harness-adf`, AI Services/Azure OpenAI, ADLS storage, Entra app registrations, Azure SQL, App Insights), Snowflake (the account, warehouses, PATs, DMF, Cortex, GET_LINEAGE, tags), and Databricks (workspace, SQL warehouse, Unity Catalog, DQX jobs, tokens). The PRODUCT keeps supporting all of these; only our own test instances go away. After teardown, dev/test is local-only (Postgres/MySQL/Trino containers, MinIO for S3, Azurite for ADLS, OpenBao, Mailpit, Ollama, local Airflow/dbt).

Task: list EVERY OPEN GitHub issue AND every OPEN pull request (`gh issue list --state open --limit 500 --json number,title,body,labels,milestone` and `gh pr list --state open --json number,title,body`), and READ EACH BODY IN FULL (plus comments where the body is ambiguous: `gh issue view N --comments`). For each item decide whether it has a DIRECT or INDIRECT dependency on AWS, Azure, Snowflake or Databricks, meaning any of:
 (a) its work needs one of those live accounts to implement or apply (IaC apply, credential, a cloud-only API);
 (b) its acceptance / verification needs a live run on one of them (e.g. "live-verify on Snowflake/UC", driver-boundary rule);
 (c) it is a decision gate or cleanup about those estates (teardown, keep/retire);
 (d) it would become impossible or unverifiable once they are gone (e.g. depends on a specific cloud feature: Cortex, DMF, DQX, GET_LINEAGE, Key Vault, Secrets Manager, Entra, Cognito, SES, Bedrock, Azure OpenAI, ADF, OneLake/Fabric, Synapse, Athena, Redshift);
 (e) it is blocked on a vendor package tied to one of them (snowflake-sqlalchemy, databricks-sql-connector, etc.).
Incidental mentions (e.g. listing Snowflake as an example) are NOT dependencies — say so.

Output (keep it tight, no preamble): a markdown table of ONLY the items with a dependency: | # | title (short) | platform(s) | type a–e | what exactly is needed | can it be done/verified BEFORE teardown today? (yes/no/partly + why) |. Then a one-line list of the numbers you judged incidental-only. Then the total counts. Be precise; quote the exact phrase from the issue that establishes the dependency for any borderline call.

